00:02:33.917 --> 00:02:34.940
Virginia Ciambriello: Hello, everyone.

00:02:35.106 --> 00:02:41.049
Today I'm here to talk you about
something that really matters to

00:02:41.049 --> 00:02:44.388
me, and the topic is accessibility.

00:02:44.831 --> 00:02:50.055
But I'm not gonna talk you
I'm not gonna talk only about

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:56.221
accessibility, but I'm gonna start
from my own story with epilepsy.

00:02:57.356 --> 00:03:02.774
First of all, it's nice to meet
you and then later talk to you.

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:04.538
My name is Virginia.

00:03:04.595 --> 00:03:07.970
I am 30 years old, and I'm a queer person.

00:03:08.013 --> 00:03:13.982
I I'm now living in Torino in
Italy, and I also live with a

00:03:14.017 --> 00:03:16.603
chronic illness that is epilepsy.

00:03:17.731 --> 00:03:24.253
I'm here today to tell you my story
because there is something that I

00:03:24.356 --> 00:03:30.200
really think that's important when
we talk about accessibility, and that

00:03:30.221 --> 00:03:37.530
thing is to understand how people with
disability and chronic illness live,

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:43.865
and the things, the obstacles they
have to face every day in their life.

00:03:45.345 --> 00:03:51.242
In Italy and in Europe especially
the last year was an important year

00:03:51.262 --> 00:03:53.779
because of the Accessibility Act.

00:03:54.399 --> 00:04:01.493
And it was very important to to see
that institution really understood

00:04:01.498 --> 00:04:06.584
the importance of accessibility
and a regulation for accessibility.

00:04:06.995 --> 00:04:14.390
But I think that the the regulations
without listening and understanding

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:20.844
people's stories are not the type and
kind of accessibility we really need,

00:04:21.193 --> 00:04:24.257
especially in digital environments.

00:04:26.209 --> 00:04:28.606
First of all what is epilepsy?

00:04:28.679 --> 00:04:34.515
I told you that I'm gonna share my
story, but I'm not only gonna share some

00:04:34.821 --> 00:04:41.623
information about how I've managed and
how I still manage my chronic illness,

00:04:41.641 --> 00:04:48.317
but I wanna give you some information
about my chronic illness because I

00:04:48.317 --> 00:04:56.149
believe that it's also important to
understand how a condition let's say,

00:04:56.223 --> 00:05:03.020
works to really understand how to ensure
an accessible digital environment.

00:05:04.085 --> 00:05:10.146
Epilepsy, first of all, is a
chronic illness, but we can't talk

00:05:10.155 --> 00:05:16.560
about epilepsy, but we should talk
about epilepsies because there

00:05:16.561 --> 00:05:20.263
is not only one kind of epilepsy.

00:05:20.916 --> 00:05:22.510
They're not the same.

00:05:23.097 --> 00:05:29.139
While there are epileptic people
in the world, all over the world,

00:05:29.522 --> 00:05:35.661
we may experience a different
kind of the same chronic illness.

00:05:36.904 --> 00:05:43.083
Epilepsy is a neurological condition
characterized by sudden seizures.

00:05:43.138 --> 00:05:49.222
In Italy, it affects about one in
100 people, so we are talking about

00:05:49.722 --> 00:05:53.885
a half million people only in Italy

00:05:56.229 --> 00:06:02.799
We  said that epilepsy is
characterized by sudden seizures.

00:06:03.505 --> 00:06:05.220
How does a seizure manifest?

00:06:05.341 --> 00:06:13.003
Because this is important for people
working in accessibility and working in

00:06:13.010 --> 00:06:22.281
digital environments as well, because
sometime even elements in the in the

00:06:22.319 --> 00:06:26.513
user interface can trigger a seizure.

00:06:27.351 --> 00:06:34.258
As for the epilepsies, we talk about
different kind of seizures because

00:06:34.724 --> 00:06:41.099
seizures are always like a brief
temporary kind of short circuit

00:06:41.243 --> 00:06:43.731
between the neurons of our brain.

00:06:44.419 --> 00:06:51.133
And you may experience absences, you
may ex- experience muscle jerks or

00:06:51.190 --> 00:06:58.232
loss of consciousness and in the most
difficult times, convulsions, Seizures

00:06:58.584 --> 00:07:06.023
are divided in general between partial
seizures and generalized seizure.

00:07:06.983 --> 00:07:16.409
While not everyone experience generalized
seizures most of the time it is

00:07:16.413 --> 00:07:25.491
well-known that  partial seizures can
affect and they do affect most of the

00:07:25.572 --> 00:07:27.720
people living with this condition.

00:07:28.715 --> 00:07:35.176
Partial seizures are seizures
that last really short time.

00:07:35.532 --> 00:07:41.405
They last for a really short amount
of time and while you could experience

00:07:41.412 --> 00:07:47.975
abnormal movements, sensory phenomena,
like for example not remembering

00:07:47.985 --> 00:07:54.586
well the name of the thing you have
in front of you or maybe you aren't

00:07:54.592 --> 00:08:01.940
able to speak properly you may not
experience a, a loss of consciousness.

00:08:02.391 --> 00:08:08.200
Loss of consciousness is as a
matter of fact a particular element

00:08:08.234 --> 00:08:13.927
of the generalized seizures that
are that kind of seizure that

00:08:14.046 --> 00:08:17.373
always comes with a convulsion.

00:08:17.715 --> 00:08:24.348
So the kind of seizure we have
also been used to see even in

00:08:24.409 --> 00:08:26.540
medical drama, for example.

00:08:26.799 --> 00:08:32.401
I don't know if every if some of you
have ever seen some kind of medical

00:08:32.410 --> 00:08:38.575
drama like ER or Grey's Anatomy to
talk about the m- most famous, but

00:08:39.072 --> 00:08:46.356
in medical dramas what we see most
of the time is a generalized seizure.

00:08:46.375 --> 00:08:53.089
So a person that comes sometimes in
the in ER with loss of consciousness,

00:08:53.091 --> 00:09:00.823
with muscle jerks with eyes that goes
beneath their head and all this kind of

00:09:00.837 --> 00:09:09.084
symptoms When we when we are designing
and taking accessibility into account

00:09:09.093 --> 00:09:16.540
for digital environment, it is important
to pay a lot of attention to partial

00:09:16.552 --> 00:09:24.146
seizure that can be triggered by some
elements in our user interface, but also

00:09:24.209 --> 00:09:27.580
to collateral effects of medication.

00:09:28.065 --> 00:09:37.839
But we will see it briefly My journey
with epilepsy started a long time ago.

00:09:38.236 --> 00:09:42.167
I always say that my epilepsy.

00:09:42.396 --> 00:09:50.100
My epilepsy this year turned adult because
it's 18 years with this chronic illness.

00:09:51.072 --> 00:09:54.595
I had my first seizure when I was 12.

00:09:54.952 --> 00:09:56.760
It was 2008.

00:09:57.973 --> 00:10:05.198
It was my first seizure, but it was
also the first time for my family to

00:10:05.300 --> 00:10:09.209
assist to someone living a seizure.

00:10:09.998 --> 00:10:18.149
From that time we were like put in
some kind of new experiencing a new

00:10:18.187 --> 00:10:22.348
kind of time time span and time zone.

00:10:22.361 --> 00:10:29.874
It was like everything was really slow and
really fast at the same time because first

00:10:29.875 --> 00:10:33.541
of all, we had to experience my recovery.

00:10:33.572 --> 00:10:40.352
It lasted 20 days in the hospital
because they had to run a lot of tests.

00:10:40.363 --> 00:10:47.437
They have to… They had to manage my
symptoms while trying to figure out

00:10:47.580 --> 00:10:52.168
what was that triggered my my seizure.

00:10:53.047 --> 00:11:01.048
After my 20 days at the hospital the
doctors told me that I had epilepsy.

00:11:01.048 --> 00:11:03.595
I was diagnosed with epilepsy.

00:11:04.173 --> 00:11:05.748
Why am I telling this?

00:11:05.778 --> 00:11:11.864
Because this is another important
element that is often misunderstood.

00:11:12.440 --> 00:11:20.306
You can experience a seizure in your
life and still not be diagnosed with

00:11:20.421 --> 00:11:27.300
epilepsy because seizures can be
elements and they can be triggered

00:11:27.709 --> 00:11:33.287
also by other kind of diseases,
disabilities, and chronic illness.

00:11:34.137 --> 00:11:41.973
They run some tests on my brain, and I
was diagnosed with this chronic illness.

00:11:42.873 --> 00:11:50.715
From 2008 to 2012, I had a lot of
convulsion and generalized seizures.

00:11:51.221 --> 00:11:59.372
I had about 10 night seizures, and I
started taking the first of what will what

00:11:59.374 --> 00:12:02.499
will be a lot of different medications.

00:12:03.145 --> 00:12:07.726
Because of this, I experienced
a lot of collateral effect.

00:12:08.220 --> 00:12:14.166
The most important and significant
one was the the fatigue and the

00:12:14.167 --> 00:12:16.165
fact that I was always sleeping.

00:12:16.567 --> 00:12:23.841
I I had started in 2009 the high
school, and so it was very difficult

00:12:23.856 --> 00:12:30.227
for me try to stay focused at school
and try to focus for my homework.

00:12:31.012 --> 00:12:41.060
In 2012 because of I was responding
to my medication one of the clinicians

00:12:41.092 --> 00:12:48.486
that was taking care of me decided to
interrupt the medication This is something

00:12:48.537 --> 00:12:54.840
common in this kind of chronic illness
because if you respond very well and if

00:12:54.840 --> 00:13:03.568
you don't have seizures for continued
amount of time you can even you can

00:13:03.610 --> 00:13:07.161
even finish taking your medication.

00:13:07.668 --> 00:13:15.040
So I stopped my medication, taking my
pills, and it was like, … I remember

00:13:15.040 --> 00:13:23.140
it as the m- most beautiful time of my
adolescence because I was really young

00:13:23.380 --> 00:13:30.910
and I was just trying to live a normal
life, act like a normal teenager, and

00:13:30.987 --> 00:13:39.687
my everyday wish was to be treated
and to be seen as a normal teenager.

00:13:39.763 --> 00:13:45.196
The absence of the symptoms the
absence of the the illness as we

00:13:45.196 --> 00:13:48.584
can say  made me really happy.

00:13:49.126 --> 00:13:53.127
But I have a relapse in 2014.

00:13:53.564 --> 00:13:57.184
So I started new therapeutic plan.

00:13:57.243 --> 00:14:01.957
We have to switch with my
medication with another type,

00:14:01.958 --> 00:14:03.846
a stronger type of medication.

00:14:04.239 --> 00:14:10.546
And I also started to taking notes,
having my therapeutic diary again.

00:14:10.925 --> 00:14:18.190
Because another important thing in this
kind of illnesses that are chronic is

00:14:18.218 --> 00:14:25.042
to take notes of all your symptoms to
understand if they change during your

00:14:25.092 --> 00:14:32.851
during the time, if they change and maybe
they're triggered by something different.

00:14:33.679 --> 00:14:40.337
From 2014 to 2019, I had
other generalized seizures.

00:14:40.889 --> 00:14:46.791
And I I stopped taking some
medication to get to another.

00:14:47.195 --> 00:14:50.404
Let's say to get to
another therapeutic plan.

00:14:51.537 --> 00:14:54.285
It was till 2019.

00:14:54.900 --> 00:15:04.804
It was February the 5th of 2019,
and it was the last day of seizures,

00:15:04.966 --> 00:15:09.149
generalized seizures for me, as for now.

00:15:09.979 --> 00:15:17.353
So I was really happy because year
after year, I was not I was no more

00:15:17.353 --> 00:15:24.115
resistant to medication, and so I
could manage a better way of living.

00:15:24.637 --> 00:15:32.564
But as the seizure stopped, the collateral
effects of my medication started.

00:15:32.893 --> 00:15:38.274
I started to have what it's
called nystagmus, and this is

00:15:38.598 --> 00:15:44.343
different kind of movement in
the eye that affects your view.

00:15:45.605 --> 00:15:51.571
This can happen, and this happens a
lot because while you're taking some

00:15:51.572 --> 00:16:00.354
medication to manage your illness and most
important the most important effects of

00:16:00.354 --> 00:16:07.758
your illness sometimes there is there is
another response of your body, and that

00:16:07.814 --> 00:16:10.953
response is in the collateral effects.

00:16:11.852 --> 00:16:20.036
I'm saying this because when we look at
at technical requirements at regulations,

00:16:20.293 --> 00:16:27.990
when we go deeper into designing for
people with disability we still have

00:16:28.480 --> 00:16:35.814
in mind that kind of disability, what
that disability means for that person.

00:16:36.185 --> 00:16:42.683
But like I said before we have
in mind an image like we were

00:16:42.724 --> 00:16:46.337
told, like we were used to learn.

00:16:46.958 --> 00:16:55.533
But I'm here today also to remember
you that there are some triggers

00:16:55.690 --> 00:17:01.966
that sometimes are hidden even
in the chronic illness that maybe

00:17:01.966 --> 00:17:04.592
we can we can, we know most.

00:17:04.712 --> 00:17:05.176
Okay?

00:17:05.553 --> 00:17:12.727
And when we are designing for people
with disabilities, we need to remind

00:17:12.852 --> 00:17:20.358
that there are also other elements
that affect their everyday experience

00:17:20.358 --> 00:17:26.661
of digital environment, and these
elements are, most of the time, the

00:17:26.664 --> 00:17:29.257
collateral effects of medication.

00:17:30.167 --> 00:17:38.753
So I started experiencing these
collateral effects, and in 2024, back

00:17:38.755 --> 00:17:43.399
to 2024, I moved with my wife to Torino.

00:17:43.683 --> 00:17:51.565
So we we came here and I started to
look for another clinician to take

00:17:51.577 --> 00:17:57.124
care of me and follow my my path
taking care of my therapeutic plan.

00:17:59.089 --> 00:18:06.321
As for this timeline as, as you
can saw my timeline was filled with

00:18:06.421 --> 00:18:10.179
different different important dates.

00:18:10.973 --> 00:18:19.057
And those dates also those dates were
were followed by different travels.

00:18:19.409 --> 00:18:27.955
I traveled a lot because I I wanted
answers, but doctors couldn't find an

00:18:27.955 --> 00:18:31.558
answer for a cause for my epilepsy.

00:18:32.023 --> 00:18:39.116
Just for your information, when you
can't find an answer and a, a specific

00:18:39.151 --> 00:18:44.655
cause for an illness or a chronic
condition, it is called idiopathic.

00:18:44.971 --> 00:18:53.292
So I went from the north to the
southern Italy to to have some answers.

00:18:53.833 --> 00:19:02.457
And at some point I I started to
accept that maybe it was really hard

00:19:02.613 --> 00:19:08.712
to understand why I why I had epilepsy.

00:19:11.253 --> 00:19:17.455
As I told you, at some time one of
the clinician that was taking care

00:19:17.457 --> 00:19:25.491
of me in Benevento he told me that I
need to cope with the nystagmus, and

00:19:25.491 --> 00:19:29.066
I had to try to live a normal life.

00:19:30.860 --> 00:19:39.390
When he told me this, I was, like, in
shock because I was already trying to

00:19:39.391 --> 00:19:45.883
live a normal life with the nystagmus, but
it was really hard because if something

00:19:45.886 --> 00:19:54.872
affects your view it means that your
view is unstable, so it affects every

00:19:54.939 --> 00:19:58.305
every path of your of your everyday life.

00:19:59.098 --> 00:20:09.860
That meant for me trying to figure out
how to manage this new kind of view and

00:20:10.320 --> 00:20:13.711
we can say new kind of eyes that I had.

00:20:15.870 --> 00:20:22.823
To let you understand what a day with
the nystagmus means, and to give you

00:20:23.202 --> 00:20:29.627
even like a a short time of testing
with a person with chronic illness.

00:20:30.437 --> 00:20:34.958
Nystagmus, as I told you, is an
abnormal movement of the eyes

00:20:35.325 --> 00:20:37.733
and it makes vision unstable.

00:20:38.211 --> 00:20:47.114
Mostly of the time it is it is made and it
is due to medication taken for epilepsy.

00:20:48.248 --> 00:20:54.746
For me ,for my everyday life, living
with the nystagmus meant taking the

00:20:54.747 --> 00:20:58.456
subways test and hoping not to fall.

00:20:58.982 --> 00:21:04.362
Zooming on the PC because the
characters were too small and

00:21:04.362 --> 00:21:07.080
they moved quickly for my eyes.

00:21:08.195 --> 00:21:15.221
Also believing that a seizure could
trigger and could come at any moment

00:21:15.321 --> 00:21:22.494
because nystagmus is really similar
to the the triggers and the things

00:21:22.495 --> 00:21:24.989
I experience before a seizure.

00:21:25.992 --> 00:21:33.586
But it also meant while I was in
university and while I was working, it

00:21:33.724 --> 00:21:40.994
meant not being able to write a message
or taking notes or write in a document.

00:21:41.882 --> 00:21:52.730
So this was the hidden effect If  if I
if maybe I spoke with someone working

00:21:52.730 --> 00:22:01.872
i- in the user interface field, UI and
UX field and I and I asked them or her

00:22:01.891 --> 00:22:09.628
or they, I asked they what was like
for them to to design for a person with

00:22:09.629 --> 00:22:17.478
epilepsy most of the times they would
have told me everything about flashes,

00:22:17.915 --> 00:22:27.474
everything about characters, but nothing
about the hidden effects that you can,

00:22:27.532 --> 00:22:35.084
you can't see sometimes, and you can't
be able to learn to and learn about

00:22:35.163 --> 00:22:41.859
until you understand and talk with the
person who lives that chronic illness.

00:22:42.966 --> 00:22:52.331
So the paradox for me was that I
had to accept a smaller but constant

00:22:52.366 --> 00:22:59.636
pain to avoid a greater pain, and
this is important for me to tell you

00:22:59.637 --> 00:23:03.591
today because this is the main point.

00:23:04.095 --> 00:23:12.564
We hardly ever consider the side
effects of a disability, but yet they

00:23:12.564 --> 00:23:19.211
are elements to take into account for
accessible design because they really

00:23:19.899 --> 00:23:26.288
outline the contours of everyday
life of a person with a disability

00:23:28.438 --> 00:23:35.790
maybe we are designing for people who have
a stable vision currently, like it was

00:23:35.817 --> 00:23:43.866
for me, or an attention span compromised
by medication or chronic fatigue also

00:23:43.907 --> 00:23:46.147
experienced because of medication.

00:23:46.702 --> 00:23:54.131
And we, as I told you, we hardly
ever consider those kind of side

00:23:54.134 --> 00:24:03.409
effects, but it is precisely in that
minute and invisible element that the

00:24:03.413 --> 00:24:10.080
contours of of the experience of a
person with a disability is outlined.

00:24:11.285 --> 00:24:18.607
If we don't take into account side
effects of medication and side effects

00:24:18.614 --> 00:24:25.784
of chronic illnesses, our accessible
design will always be incomplete

00:24:28.270 --> 00:24:35.649
As I was saying to you, I especially
during my teenage years, I had to

00:24:35.650 --> 00:24:43.385
face a lot of physical barriers,
but also a lot of social barriers.

00:24:44.175 --> 00:24:50.935
When we talk when we talk about the
barriers and the world we live in

00:24:50.957 --> 00:24:59.272
that is meant … sometimes it seems
like it's meant to to avoid people

00:24:59.281 --> 00:25:07.421
with disability and avoid them from
a from a, a great experience of of

00:25:07.466 --> 00:25:10.353
their life and their also digital life.

00:25:11.218 --> 00:25:17.516
Into these barriers we have to take,
of course, into account also digital

00:25:17.535 --> 00:25:25.384
barriers, especially for people with
disability, because people with disability

00:25:25.390 --> 00:25:33.605
sometimes experience isolation and it
is well known that a lot of people with

00:25:33.643 --> 00:25:40.636
chronic conditions and disability started
to feel more at their ease and started to

00:25:40.641 --> 00:25:48.482
advocate for themselves through digital
tools, through blogs through blog, blogs

00:25:48.495 --> 00:25:55.057
made with with WordPress, for example, as
it was for me through the social media.

00:25:55.354 --> 00:26:01.863
And so it is important that
we take into accounts how to

00:26:01.867 --> 00:26:04.638
avoid those digital barriers.

00:26:06.336 --> 00:26:14.257
What and which are digital barriers that
people with epilepsy face every day?

00:26:15.025 --> 00:26:17.437
First of all, the low contrast.

00:26:17.441 --> 00:26:23.116
Sometimes it is important for us
to have a high contrast because

00:26:23.431 --> 00:26:30.116
different kind of lights and even,
for example, sunlight can trigger us.

00:26:31.079 --> 00:26:37.082
There are also the filters that are
used inappropriately, for example,

00:26:37.113 --> 00:26:39.702
flashing lights put at maximum.

00:26:40.582 --> 00:26:48.631
But also something  related precisely
to user experience, for example, the

00:26:48.690 --> 00:26:55.247
website navigation the text that are
too small, text in the buttons that

00:26:55.249 --> 00:27:02.749
are too small, or  aesthetic elements
that are put in the website because

00:27:02.785 --> 00:27:10.940
we prefer an aesthetic website instead
of an accessible and aesthetic one.

00:27:11.666 --> 00:27:14.318
Like for example, moving backgrounds.

00:27:14.614 --> 00:27:21.990
When you have a moving backgrounds in
front of you and you have to read the

00:27:22.038 --> 00:27:30.803
H1, H2 titles believe me, it is very
difficult because the vision is unstable.

00:27:30.872 --> 00:27:36.235
And those elements, even if
they're not flashing, but they're

00:27:36.239 --> 00:27:41.671
moving really quickly, they can
also trigger partial seizures.

00:27:42.840 --> 00:27:46.052
And then there are the
triggers we know the most.

00:27:46.052 --> 00:27:51.531
The triggers we also we also
see mentioned in the WCAG.

00:27:51.579 --> 00:27:54.341
That are the photosensitivity triggers.

00:27:55.489 --> 00:28:00.731
M- For example, the fast transitions
in videos, the lights that are too

00:28:00.744 --> 00:28:06.915
bright overlapping text in background
videos, and low contrast in images.

00:28:07.908 --> 00:28:17.439
These are specific triggers that
are related to one type of epilepsy.

00:28:17.772 --> 00:28:21.507
That is photosensitivity epilepsy.

00:28:22.524 --> 00:28:30.337
Me, for example, I experience I experience
hard barriers with low vision sometime,

00:28:30.427 --> 00:28:36.958
but sometimes, but I'm not a person
with a photosensitivity epilepsy.

00:28:38.149 --> 00:28:39.836
Why am I telling you this?

00:28:39.858 --> 00:28:47.184
Because once again even in the technical
part of the of the the accessible

00:28:47.496 --> 00:28:53.649
elements regulation, we take into
account a general el- element, but

00:28:54.018 --> 00:28:56.987
it is important for us to go deeper.

00:28:57.727 --> 00:29:06.870
And what it is important to understand is
that when we design a chaotic interface

00:29:06.889 --> 00:29:15.074
because our client told us that it- he
saw it into another website and it was

00:29:15.079 --> 00:29:22.969
fancy and it was the best of the best,
or maybe we we are tired and we really

00:29:22.994 --> 00:29:30.017
don't want to check the contrast, we
believe that the contrast is okay, we

00:29:30.081 --> 00:29:32.854
aren't just delivering a bad design.

00:29:33.390 --> 00:29:43.132
We are we are giving the space to
invisible walls that prevent, for

00:29:43.132 --> 00:29:50.163
example, in Italy, half a million
people to, from navigating safely.

00:29:52.602 --> 00:30:00.176
So to come to a practical solution
it is fortune we don't have to

00:30:00.176 --> 00:30:02.627
invent anything from scratch.

00:30:03.082 --> 00:30:11.567
The WCAG dedicate a specific attention
to this part under the second

00:30:11.586 --> 00:30:13.748
principle, the operable principle.

00:30:14.654 --> 00:30:20.410
There is an entire section,
the 2.3, dedicated to seizure

00:30:20.734 --> 00:30:23.052
and physical reactions.

00:30:23.109 --> 00:30:29.935
What is important to to follow as
criterias of success for the WCAG?

00:30:30.676 --> 00:30:36.463
The first thing is the three flashes
or below the threshold, but we are

00:30:36.507 --> 00:30:44.857
talking about level A. Avoid, if we
wanna go to the triple A level, avoid

00:30:44.885 --> 00:30:48.078
more than three flashes in one second.

00:30:48.086 --> 00:30:54.584
But for experience, in my experience,
I suggest you to when it's possible,

00:30:54.626 --> 00:31:02.392
to avoid flashes or at least to
put flashes in a more longer, in

00:31:02.393 --> 00:31:05.751
a longer span five, 10 seconds.

00:31:06.984 --> 00:31:12.156
And then the point of
animation from interactions.

00:31:12.686 --> 00:31:19.934
We have to, we need to be able to stop
some interactions or some animations

00:31:19.939 --> 00:31:30.904
whenever we need, if we need it But
if till now we said that designing

00:31:30.928 --> 00:31:37.616
for chronic illness and designing
for epilepsy means not only stay on

00:31:37.626 --> 00:31:42.502
the surface, but also taking into
account, for example, side effects.

00:31:43.446 --> 00:31:51.924
It also means to check the contrast
and maintain a, a contrast of 4.5:1

00:31:52.248 --> 00:31:55.787
in the visual representation of text.

00:31:56.592 --> 00:32:03.358
To use legible and clear fonts
for diplopia and nystagmus.

00:32:04.223 --> 00:32:09.365
If we need to put text into
a video, let it be legible.

00:32:09.846 --> 00:32:17.765
And also cursors in the navigation,
they have to be clearly visible.

00:32:18.701 --> 00:32:25.226
Another important thing is the ability
to pause the content that has animation,

00:32:25.279 --> 00:32:27.416
movements, that has interaction.

00:32:27.711 --> 00:32:34.854
So let's not let's not do that fancy
thing that video plays in automatic when

00:32:34.867 --> 00:32:38.084
we when we are designing some pages.

00:32:41.426 --> 00:32:48.897
Accessibility for me as a person with
epilepsy and also as a a consultant on

00:32:48.899 --> 00:32:57.276
accessibility and on clear communication,
it's just not compliance with guidelines.

00:32:57.817 --> 00:33:03.399
Because if we stick to guidelines,
we are missing the focus.

00:33:03.681 --> 00:33:07.391
We are missing the living
experience of people.

00:33:07.422 --> 00:33:13.470
So we have to listen, we have to
question ourself and understand

00:33:13.472 --> 00:33:16.834
if someone gives us a feedback.

00:33:17.313 --> 00:33:25.523
So when we start designing, accessibility
must be already in the first place.

00:33:25.834 --> 00:33:32.159
Because when we talk about accessibility,
we aren't talking about a plugin inserted

00:33:32.177 --> 00:33:38.813
into a website, but we are talking
about guaranteeing people the ex- the

00:33:38.866 --> 00:33:45.572
ability to experience the world and the
digital world according to their needs.

00:33:47.477 --> 00:33:54.653
To, to come to an end my, my experience
of a person living with epilepsy

00:33:54.974 --> 00:34:01.266
lives in a paradox because it's a
point that intersect perfectly the

00:34:01.278 --> 00:34:03.526
visible and invisible illnesses.

00:34:03.886 --> 00:34:08.587
Because my illness is visible to
others when I lose consciousness

00:34:08.590 --> 00:34:10.064
and so I can't see it.

00:34:10.153 --> 00:34:14.028
I'm not a witness of
what is happening to me.

00:34:14.408 --> 00:34:21.642
But every day when I do see it in the
digital obstacles, in the fatigue,

00:34:21.875 --> 00:34:27.815
in the chronic fatigue and in the
nystagmus or in the diplopia, other,

00:34:28.192 --> 00:34:33.840
others forget that it exists because I'm
here, because I'm healthy, because I'm

00:34:33.840 --> 00:34:37.617
not having a seizure as they know it.

00:34:39.322 --> 00:34:46.473
So design with people who cannot
look at a flashing screen in mind.

00:34:47.118 --> 00:34:53.420
Please test your products by asking
neurodivergent peoples for feedback and

00:34:53.433 --> 00:35:01.243
make sure the web is a place where no
one has to be afraid of making a click.

00:35:02.888 --> 00:35:04.132
Thank you so much.

